@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Resolver

@s From Parsed Occurrences to Definitions

Parser preserved every code-block occurrence in the `Greeting` example. Book
may place several parsed chapters into the same @code{Program}, but it likewise
leaves their names uncombined. We begin with one file and extend its source
with three more occurrences of `Greeting line`:

```text
@title A Greeting
@s Greeting
This small program writes one line.

--- hello.txt
@{Greeting line}
---

The text comes from a separate named piece.

--- Greeting line
Hello
---

--- Greeting line +=
from Litweb
---

--- Greeting line :=
Good morning
---

--- Greeting line +=
from Litweb
---
```

Parser returns the two unmodified occurrences as **bases**. The other three
are **changes**: `+=` marks an addition and `:=` marks a replacement.

```text
parsed code occurrences
|-- base         "hello.txt"       line 5
|-- base         "Greeting line"   line 11
|-- addition     "Greeting line"   line 15
|-- replacement  "Greeting line"   line 19
`-- addition     "Greeting line"   line 23
```

Resolver combines occurrences that share a name. It creates one final
definition for each base, finds the base affected by each change, and applies
the changes in source order.

@s The Resolved Greeting

The first addition temporarily leaves `Hello` followed by `from Litweb`. The
replacement discards both lines and supplies `Good morning`; the last addition
then appends a new `from Litweb`. The result has two definitions rather than
five occurrences:

```text
ResolvedProgram
|-- blocks[0] "hello.txt"
|   `-- "@{Greeting line}"        source line 6
|-- blocks[1] "Greeting line"
|   |-- "Good morning"            source line 20
|   `-- "from Litweb"             source line 24
`-- roots[0] -> blocks[0] -> "hello.txt"
```

The positions `0` and `1` are stable **definition identities**. Roots and
name lookups can point to those small indices without copying a definition.
`hello.txt` is a **root**, a definition selected to name a complete output
file. `Greeting line` is a supporting definition.

The whole-line `@{Greeting line}` is a **use**. Resolver deliberately leaves
it as line text. It builds the lookup information by which Tangler can later
select and expand the definition and Weaver can link the use.

@s Resolving in Two Passes

Resolution uses two passes. The first pass walks the parsed occurrences in
source order:

1. `hello.txt` becomes `blocks[0]` and `roots[0]`, and its name maps to block
   index 0.
2. The base `Greeting line` becomes `blocks[1]`, with its name mapped to index
   1.
3. The addition, replacement, and last addition are retained for later.

The second pass applies those retained changes in their source order. For
`blocks[1]`, the line state changes from `Hello`, to `Hello` plus
`from Litweb`, to `Good morning`, and finally to the two lines shown in the
resolved tree.

This separation matters because Litweb permits a change to appear before its
base. By the second pass, every valid base and lookup entry is already known,
but the changes still run in the order authored.

--- Resolve program
pub fn resolve(program: &Program) -> Result<ResolvedProgram, ResolveErrors> {
    @{Initialize resolution indexes}
    @{Collect definitions roots and changes}
    @{Apply changes in source order}
    @{Finish resolution}
}
---

@s The Resolver Module

The generated module owns the resolved model, the @code{resolve} operation,
name lookup, root recognition, portable path validation, and the semantic
errors that can prevent a complete result.

--- src/resolver.rs
