---

Resolver uses hash maps only for name and path selection; ordered vectors
retain the source and root order. Path components support portable validation,
and Parser supplies the located occurrences that Resolver receives.

--- Resolver imports
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::fmt;
use std::path::{Component, Path, PathBuf};

use crate::parser::{BlockKind, Line, Modifier, Program, SourceOrigin};
---

@s Definitions, Changes, Uses, and Roots

The example illustrates the four roles. A base creates a definition identity.
An addition appends lines to that definition; a redefinition replaces its
current lines. A use is a whole-line reference that a later stage can look up.
A root is an ordinary definition that also supplies a validated relative
output path.

The first `Greeting line` occurrence is therefore both a parsed block and the
base of `blocks[1]`. Its three changes remain separate parsed occurrences, but
they do not receive more positions in @code{ResolvedProgram.blocks}. Their
surviving lines become part of the one resolved definition.

@code{ChangeKind} lets the two-pass implementation and its diagnostics carry
the difference between addition and replacement explicitly.

--- Kinds of definition change
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ChangeKind {
    Addition,
    Redefinition,
}

impl fmt::Display for ChangeKind {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            Self::Addition => f.write_str("add to"),
            Self::Redefinition => f.write_str("redefine"),
        }
    }
}
---

@s The Resolved Model

@code{ResolvedBlock} stores one accepted base and its final lines. In the
running example the two values appear in the source order of the
`hello.txt` and `Greeting line` bases. Changes update lines but never insert
another definition identity. The base's comment pattern and @code{noHeader}
choice travel with the final definition for Tangler.

--- Resolved definitions
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ResolvedBlock {
    pub chapter: usize,
    pub name: String,
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub lines: Vec<Line>,
    pub(crate) line_chapters: Vec<usize>,
    pub comment_string: String,
    pub no_header: bool,
}
---

A root does not copy its definition. It points to one stable block index and
adds the validated path and the origin of the base that requested the output.
Thus `roots[0].block == 0` selects the `hello.txt` definition shown above.

--- Resolved roots
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ResolvedRoot {
    pub block: usize,
    pub path: PathBuf,
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
}
---

The complete program owns those ordered definitions and roots. It also owns
one local name map per chapter and a book-wide map from each name to all
matching block indices. Those maps answer selection questions without
controlling the order of definitions or outputs.

--- Resolved program indexes
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ResolvedProgram {
    pub blocks: Vec<ResolvedBlock>,
    pub roots: Vec<ResolvedRoot>,
    local_lookup: Vec<HashMap<String, usize>>,
    by_name: HashMap<String, Vec<usize>>,
}
---

@s Discovering Output Roots

The running example's `hello.txt` base becomes a root, so `roots[0]` points to
`blocks[0]`. Root status belongs to the base identity. A change may alter the
root's final lines, but a change cannot create another root.

Litweb recognizes roots by this filename convention:

- `--- src/main.rs` is an inferred root;
- `--- nested/output.txt` is another inferred root;
- `--- "Makefile"` is an explicitly quoted extensionless root; and
- `--- notes.v1/Algorithm` is not a root merely because its directory has a dot.

Quoting requests a root explicitly. For an unquoted name, Litweb inspects only
the final path component for a nonempty extension. This convention cannot
prove the author's intention: a descriptive name such as
`Algorithm version 1.2` is also inferred to be a root. Weaver keeps the same
ordered root list because it displays root definitions in bold even when only
HTML is requested.

--- Recognize a root name
fn is_root_name(name: &str, quoted: bool) -> bool {
    quoted
        || Path::new(name)
            .file_name()
            .and_then(|filename| Path::new(filename).extension())
            .is_some_and(|extension| !extension.is_empty())
}
---

@s Portable Root Paths

A root may name a file inside the chosen output directory, but it may not
escape that directory or depend on platform-specific spelling.
`generated/main.rs` and `Makefile` are accepted. Absolute paths, `..` or `.`
components, drive prefixes, backslashes, and trailing separators are rejected
before expansion.

Resolver checks the path components exactly as written. It does not inspect
the filesystem or follow symbolic links. The selected output directory is
therefore trusted; this check prevents unsafe or nonportable source spellings,
not hostile links already present on disk. Book chapter paths reuse the same
validator.

--- Validate a portable relative path
pub fn validate_relative_path(name: &str) -> Result<PathBuf, InvalidPathReason> {
    if name.is_empty() {
        return Err(InvalidPathReason::Empty);
    }
    if name.ends_with(['/', '\\']) {
        return Err(InvalidPathReason::TrailingSeparator);
    }
    if name.contains('\\') {
        return Err(InvalidPathReason::Backslash);
    }
    let bytes = name.as_bytes();
    if bytes.len() >= 2 && bytes[0].is_ascii_alphabetic() && bytes[1] == b':' {
        return Err(InvalidPathReason::WindowsPrefix);
    }

    let path = Path::new(name);
    if path.is_absolute() {
        return Err(InvalidPathReason::Absolute);
    }

    let mut normalized = PathBuf::new();
    for component in path.components() {
        match component {
            Component::Normal(component) => normalized.push(component),
            Component::ParentDir => return Err(InvalidPathReason::ParentTraversal),
            Component::CurDir => return Err(InvalidPathReason::CurrentDirectory),
            Component::RootDir => return Err(InvalidPathReason::Absolute),
            Component::Prefix(_) => return Err(InvalidPathReason::WindowsPrefix),
        }
    }
    if normalized.as_os_str().is_empty() {
        Err(InvalidPathReason::Empty)
    } else {
        Ok(normalized)
    }
}
---

@s Initializing Resolution Indexes

Resolution begins with empty ordered result vectors and one local name map per
chapter. A book-wide name index supports fallback lookup, while a path map
detects two roots that request the same destination. The retained-change list
will receive the three changes from the `Greeting` example.

The error list and sequence counter support deterministic diagnostics without
changing the order of successful definitions or roots.

--- Initialize resolution indexes
let mut blocks = Vec::new();
let mut roots = Vec::new();
let mut local_lookup = vec![HashMap::new(); program.chapters.len()];
let mut by_name: HashMap<String, Vec<usize>> = HashMap::new();
let mut root_paths = HashMap::new();
let mut changes = Vec::new();
let mut errors = Vec::new();
let mut error_sequence = 0;
---

@s Collecting Definitions, Roots, and Changes

The first pass visits chapters, semantic sections, and their code blocks in
source order; prose blocks are skipped. For each base, its current
@code{blocks.len()} becomes the stable identity inserted into the local and
book-wide maps. The block's authored lines become the initial resolved lines.

For `hello.txt`, root recognition also validates the path and records
`roots[0]`. When the pass reaches the three modified `Greeting line`
occurrences, it retains their chapter, parsed block, code metadata, and change
kind instead of creating more definitions.

Invalid bases or paths add ordered errors, but they do not disturb the source
order of accepted definitions.

--- Collect definitions roots and changes
for (chapter_index, chapter) in program.chapters.iter().enumerate() {
    for section in &chapter.sections {
        for block in &section.blocks {
            let BlockKind::Code(code) = &block.kind else {
                continue;
            };
            let addition = code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::Additive);
            let redefinition = code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::Redefinition);
            if addition || redefinition {
                let change = if addition {
                    ChangeKind::Addition
                } else {
                    ChangeKind::Redefinition
                };
                if code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::NoHeader) {
                    push_ordered_error(
                        &mut errors,
                        &mut error_sequence,
                        chapter_index,
                        ResolveError {
                            origin: block.origin.clone(),
                            kind: ResolveErrorKind::IncompatibleModifier {
                                name: code.name.clone(),
                                modifier: "noHeader".to_owned(),
                            },
                        },
                    );
                }
                changes.push((chapter_index, block, code, change));
                continue;
            }

            if local_lookup[chapter_index].contains_key(&code.name) {
                push_ordered_error(
                    &mut errors,
                    &mut error_sequence,
                    chapter_index,
                    ResolveError {
                        origin: block.origin.clone(),
                        kind: ResolveErrorKind::DuplicateDefinition {
                            name: code.name.clone(),
                        },
                    },
                );
                continue;
            }

            let index = blocks.len();
            local_lookup[chapter_index].insert(code.name.clone(), index);
            by_name.entry(code.name.clone()).or_default().push(index);
            blocks.push(ResolvedBlock {
                chapter: chapter_index,
                name: code.name.clone(),
                origin: block.origin.clone(),
                lines: block.lines.clone(),
                line_chapters: vec![chapter_index; block.lines.len()],
                comment_string: code.comment_string.clone(),
                no_header: code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::NoHeader),
            });

            if is_root_name(&code.name, code.quoted_name) {
                match validate_relative_path(&code.name) {
                    Ok(path) => {
                        if root_paths
                            .insert(path.clone(), block.origin.clone())
                            .is_some()
                        {
                            push_ordered_error(
                                &mut errors,
                                &mut error_sequence,
                                chapter_index,
                                ResolveError {
                                    origin: block.origin.clone(),
                                    kind: ResolveErrorKind::DuplicateRootPath {
                                        path,
                                    },
                                },
                            );
                        } else {
                            roots.push(ResolvedRoot {
                                block: index,
                                path,
                                origin: block.origin.clone(),
                            });
                        }
                    }
                    Err(reason) => push_ordered_error(
                        &mut errors,
                        &mut error_sequence,
                        chapter_index,
                        ResolveError {
                            origin: block.origin.clone(),
                            kind: ResolveErrorKind::InvalidRootPath {
                                path: PathBuf::from(&code.name),
                                reason,
                            },
                        },
                    ),
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

---

@s Applying Changes in Source Order

The second pass looks up each retained change. In the one-chapter example all
three select `blocks[1]`. An addition extends the current lines, while a
replacement assigns a new line vector. The same operation extends or replaces
the parallel record of which chapter supplied each line.

A missing or ambiguous target records an error and leaves the accepted
definitions available for the remaining checks, but no partial resolved
program will be returned.

--- Apply changes in source order
for (chapter_index, block, code, change) in changes {
    let index = match lookup_block(&local_lookup, &by_name, chapter_index, &code.name)
    {
        BlockLookup::Found(index) => index,
        BlockLookup::Missing => {
            push_ordered_error(
                &mut errors,
                &mut error_sequence,
                chapter_index,
                ResolveError {
                    origin: block.origin.clone(),
                    kind: ResolveErrorKind::MissingChangeTarget {
                        name: code.name.clone(),
                        change,
                    },
                },
            );
            continue;
        }
        BlockLookup::Ambiguous(indices) => {
            push_ordered_error(
                &mut errors,
                &mut error_sequence,
                chapter_index,
                ResolveError {
                    origin: block.origin.clone(),
                    kind: ResolveErrorKind::AmbiguousChangeTarget {
                        name: code.name.clone(),
                        change,
                        candidates: indices
                            .iter()
                            .map(|index| blocks[*index].origin.clone())
                            .collect(),
                    },
                },
            );
            continue;
        }
    };
    match change {
        ChangeKind::Addition => {
            blocks[index].lines.extend(block.lines.clone());
            blocks[index]
                .line_chapters
                .extend(std::iter::repeat_n(chapter_index, block.lines.len()));
        }
        ChangeKind::Redefinition => {
            blocks[index].lines = block.lines.clone();
            blocks[index].line_chapters = vec![chapter_index; block.lines.len()];
        }
    }
}

---

@s Finishing Resolution

Only a completely valid program crosses this boundary. The `Greeting` example
returns its two blocks, one root, and lookup maps. Otherwise all errors are
ordered by chapter, source line, and discovery order and returned as one
collection.

--- Finish resolution
if errors.is_empty() {
    Ok(ResolvedProgram {
        blocks,
        roots,
        local_lookup,
        by_name,
    })
} else {
    errors.sort_by_key(|(chapter, line, sequence, _)| (*chapter, *line, *sequence));
    Err(ResolveErrors {
        errors: errors.into_iter().map(|(_, _, _, error)| error).collect(),
    })
}
---

@s Extending Lookup Across a Book

For one file, every resolved line belongs to chapter zero and every name has
at most one base. A book adds a choice: a common helper name may be defined
separately in several chapters.

Suppose the English chapter contains:

```text
@s Greeting
--- hello.txt
Hello
---
--- Punctuation
!
---
```

The Spanish chapter contains its own `Punctuation` and a change to the
English root:

```text
@s Saludo
--- Punctuation
¡
---
--- hello.txt +=
@{Punctuation}
---
```

Spanish has no local `hello.txt`, so its change finds the one definition with
that name elsewhere in the book. The added reference has a different lookup
story: Spanish authored the line, so Tangler later selects Spanish's local
`Punctuation`, not the equally named English definition.

The combined root therefore carries two kinds of chapter information:

```text
ResolvedBlock "hello.txt"          base chapter: English
|-- "Hello"                        line chapter: English
`-- "@{Punctuation}"               line chapter: Spanish
```

@code{ResolvedBlock.chapter} records the base chapter. The parallel
@code{lines} and @code{line_chapters} vectors always have equal length, and
each chapter entry records who authored the line at the same position.

@s The Local-First Rule

Lookup follows four cases:

- a definition in the line's chapter wins;
- with no local definition, one definition elsewhere is the unique fallback;
- with no definition anywhere, the result is missing; and
- two or more definitions elsewhere are ambiguous.

Candidate identities retain manifest and source order, so an ambiguity is
reported deterministically. @code{block_named} succeeds only for a globally
unique name. The general @code{lookup} method returns @code{Found},
@code{Missing}, or @code{Ambiguous}.

Resolver uses this rule immediately for additions and replacements. It does
not scan `@{Name}` lines for missing or ambiguous uses. Tangler and Weaver call
@code{lookup} later when they consume those lines.

--- Resolved name lookup
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum BlockLookup {
    Found(usize),
    Missing,
    Ambiguous(Vec<usize>),
}

impl ResolvedProgram {
    pub fn block(&self, index: usize) -> &ResolvedBlock {
        &self.blocks[index]
    }

    pub fn block_named(&self, name: &str) -> Option<(usize, &ResolvedBlock)> {
        match self.by_name.get(name).map(Vec::as_slice) {
            Some([index]) => Some((*index, &self.blocks[*index])),
            _ => None,
        }
    }

    pub fn lookup(&self, chapter: usize, name: &str) -> BlockLookup {
        if let Some(index) = self
            .local_lookup
            .get(chapter)
            .and_then(|lookup| lookup.get(name))
        {
            return BlockLookup::Found(*index);
        }
        match self.by_name.get(name).map(Vec::as_slice) {
            None | Some([]) => BlockLookup::Missing,
            Some([index]) => BlockLookup::Found(*index),
            Some(indices) => BlockLookup::Ambiguous(indices.to_vec()),
        }
    }
}
---

@s Looking Up During Resolution

The construction-time helper implements the same local-first rule using the
maps before the final @code{ResolvedProgram} exists.

--- Look up a definition during resolution
fn lookup_block(
    local_lookup: &[HashMap<String, usize>],
    by_name: &HashMap<String, Vec<usize>>,
    chapter: usize,
    name: &str,
) -> BlockLookup {
    if let Some(index) = local_lookup
        .get(chapter)
        .and_then(|lookup| lookup.get(name))
    {
        return BlockLookup::Found(*index);
    }
    match by_name.get(name).map(Vec::as_slice) {
        None | Some([]) => BlockLookup::Missing,
        Some([index]) => BlockLookup::Found(*index),
        Some(indices) => BlockLookup::Ambiguous(indices.to_vec()),
    }
}
---

@s Resolution Diagnostics

Resolution reports semantic failures that Parser cannot settle: duplicate
bases in a constructed model, a missing or ambiguous change target,
incompatible modifiers on a change, invalid root paths, and duplicate output
destinations. Each error keeps its source origin and any candidate definitions
needed to explain an ambiguity.

Errors are finally ordered by chapter and source line. Discovery sequence is
an explicit tie-breaker when several failures occur on the same line.

--- Record a resolution error in discovery order
fn push_ordered_error(
    errors: &mut Vec<(usize, usize, usize, ResolveError)>,
    sequence: &mut usize,
    chapter: usize,
    error: ResolveError,
) {
    errors.push((chapter, error.origin.line, *sequence, error));
    *sequence += 1;
}
---

The structured error kind preserves those details for library callers.

--- Resolution error model
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ResolveError {
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub kind: ResolveErrorKind,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ResolveErrorKind {
    DuplicateDefinition {
        name: String,
    },
    MissingChangeTarget {
        name: String,
        change: ChangeKind,
    },
    AmbiguousChangeTarget {
        name: String,
        change: ChangeKind,
        candidates: Vec<SourceOrigin>,
    },
    IncompatibleModifier {
        name: String,
        modifier: String,
    },
    InvalidRootPath {
        path: PathBuf,
        reason: InvalidPathReason,
    },
    DuplicateRootPath {
        path: PathBuf,
    },
}
---

Path validation likewise returns a structured rejection reason that Resolver
can place inside a source-located error.

--- Portable path rejection reasons
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum InvalidPathReason {
    Empty,
    Absolute,
    ParentTraversal,
    CurrentDirectory,
    TrailingSeparator,
    Backslash,
    WindowsPrefix,
}

impl fmt::Display for InvalidPathReason {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            Self::Empty => f.write_str("the path is empty"),
            Self::Absolute => f.write_str("absolute paths are not allowed"),
            Self::ParentTraversal => f.write_str("parent traversal is not allowed"),
            Self::CurrentDirectory => {
                f.write_str("current-directory components are not allowed")
            }
            Self::TrailingSeparator => {
                f.write_str("the path ends with a directory separator")
            }
            Self::Backslash => f.write_str("backslash paths are not portable"),
            Self::WindowsPrefix => f.write_str("Windows path prefixes are not allowed"),
        }
    }
}
---

The display form adds that origin and turns each kind into a concise boundary
message.

--- Render one resolution error
impl fmt::Display for ResolveError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}: error: ", self.origin)?;
        match &self.kind {
            ResolveErrorKind::DuplicateDefinition { name } => {
                write!(f, "duplicate definition of {{{name}}}")
            }
            ResolveErrorKind::MissingChangeTarget { name, change } => {
                write!(f, "cannot {change} {{{name}}} because it is not defined")
            }
            ResolveErrorKind::AmbiguousChangeTarget {
                name,
                change,
                candidates,
            } => {
                write!(
                    f,
                    "cannot {} {{{}}} because it is ambiguous; definitions are at {}",
                    change,
                    name,
                    candidates
                        .iter()
                        .map(ToString::to_string)
                        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
                        .join(", ")
                )
            }
            ResolveErrorKind::IncompatibleModifier { name, modifier } => {
                write!(
                    f,
                    "modifier {modifier} does not apply to the change block {{{name}}}"
                )
            }
            ResolveErrorKind::InvalidRootPath { path, reason } => {
                write!(f, "invalid root path {}: {reason}", path.display())
            }
            ResolveErrorKind::DuplicateRootPath { path } => {
                write!(f, "more than one root writes {}", path.display())
            }
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for ResolveError {}
---

Resolver returns all discovered failures together. The aggregate exposes the
complete ordered collection; its short display shows the first error and
reports how many remain.

--- Resolution error collection
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ResolveErrors {
    errors: Vec<ResolveError>,
}

impl ResolveErrors {
    pub fn as_slice(&self) -> &[ResolveError] {
        &self.errors
    }

    pub fn into_vec(self) -> Vec<ResolveError> {
        self.errors
    }
}

impl fmt::Display for ResolveErrors {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        if let Some(first) = self.errors.first() {
            write!(f, "{first}")?;
            if self.errors.len() > 1 {
                write!(f, " (and {} more resolution errors)", self.errors.len() - 1)?;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for ResolveErrors {}
---

@s Handoff to Tangler and Weaver

For the running example, Resolver hands forward two final definitions,
`hello.txt` as the one ordered root, and lookup entries that connect
`Greeting line` to `blocks[1]`. The surviving reference in `hello.txt` is
still the exact source line `@{Greeting line}`.

Tangler will follow that reference and assemble `Good morning` followed by
`from Litweb` as the authored bytes of `hello.txt`. Weaver will connect the
same stable identity to the parsed base, addition, replacement, final
addition, and visible use. In a book, both stages also receive the authoring
chapter beside every final line.

Neither stage needs to collect bases, apply changes, discover roots, or rebuild
the lookup indexes.
